For how many positive integral values of $a$ is it true that $x = 2$ is the only positive integer solution of the system of inequalities $$
\begin{cases}
2x>3x-3\\
3x-a>-6
\end{cases}
$$

accepted_response:
Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, $2+3i$. Let the difference between a number and its conjugate be $2a$, so \begin{align*}
\dfrac{5+12i}{2- 3i}&=\dfrac{5+12i}{2- 3i}\times\dfrac{2+3i}{2+3i}=\dfrac{10+15i+24i-36}{2^2-(-3)^2}
=\frac{-26+39i}{4+9}\\
&=\frac{-26}{4+9}+\frac{39}{4+9}i=\boxed{-\frac{26}{13}+\frac{39}{13}i}.
\end{align*}
