Given that the graphs of a function and its inverse intersect at a point, that point must lie on the line $y=x$. Thus,
\begin{align*}
a&=f(-3)\\
&=3(-3)+b\\
&=-9+b
\end{align*}
Thus, we must have $a=b-9$. Since the graphs of $f(x)$ and $f^{-1}(x)$ intersect at a point whose x-coordinate is $-3$, the y-coordinate of that point must equal $a$. Therefore, $f^{-1}(-3)=a$. In other words,
\begin{align*}
f^{-1}(-3)&=a\\
-3&=f(a)
\end{align*}
$f(a)$ means somewhere on the original line. The line passes through the point $(-3,a)$, so the y-coordinate of that point, which is $a$, is equal to $f(-3)$. Then $-3=a$, so $a=-3$.
