In the geoboard shown, the points are evenly spaced vertically and horizontally. Segment $AB$ is drawn using two points, as shown. Point $C$ is to be chosen from the remaining $23$ points. How many of these $23$ points will result in triangle $ABC$ being isosceles? [asy]
draw((0,0)--(0,6)--(6,6)--(6,0)--cycle,linewidth(1));
for(int i=1;i<6;++i)
{for(int j=1;j<6;++j)
{dot((i,j));}
}
draw((2,2)--(4,2),linewidth(1));
label("A",(2,2),SW);
label("B",(4,2),SE);
[/asy]

accepted_response:
Let us call any pair of two points that can be chosen as $A$ and $C$. Then $B$ and $D$ can be chosen in $1$ way if the chord $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{CD}$ intersect (meaning they are either not adjacent nor exactly separated by one point). Since $\overline{AB} \cup \overline{CD}$ can be chosen in $\binom{4}{2} = 6$ ways, all we need to do is compute the probability that $\overline{AB} \cup \overline{CD}$ is none of the $6$ pairs of non_intersecting or adjacent chords. There is exactly one way in which $\overline{AB}$ intersects $\overline{CD}$ for each of the $4$ choices of $C$, and thus $\mathbb{P}(\overline{AB} \cap\overline{CD}) = \boxed{\frac{1}{3}}$.

Checking:
There are $\binom{4}{2} = 6$ ways of choosing two chords from four. Since $\overline{AB}$ intersects $\overline{CD}$ if and only if $\overline{CD}$ intersects $\overline{AB}$, exactly half of the $6$ pairs have intersecting chords. This means that $\mathbb{P}(\text{intersect}) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{3} = \frac{1}{6}$ and $\mathbb{P}(\text{NOT intersect}) = \frac{5}{6}$. So it makes sense that the answer is $\mathbb{P}(\text{intersect}) = \frac{1}{6}$. [asy]
size(12.5kt);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));
dotfactor=3;
pair A=(0,0), B=(1.5,1), C=(1.8,4), D=(0.4,4);
pair[][] dots = {{0.55A,1.13B}, {-0.42B,-.88A}, {-.26B,0.3}, {0.85B,-.25A}};
pair[][] labels = {{"$A$","$B$"}, {"$C$","$D$"},"};

//Vectors[1] lines;
for (int i=0; i<dots.length; ++i) {
for (int j=0; j<dots.length; ++j) {
for (int k=0; k<dots.length; ++k) {
for (int l=0; l<dots.length; ++l) {
if (linesLine[0][0] == i && linesLine[1][0] == j && linesLine[2][0]==k && linesLine[3][0] == l) {
dot(dots[0][j]);
} else {
label(labels[0][0]);
}
}
}
}
}
draw(A--((.5^.5)*B)--(1.5*B)--(3/2)*C--D--(1/2)*D);
[/asy]
</think> <answer>\frac{1}{3}</answer>

