Since $AB$ is the hypotenuse of right triangle $ABC$ we have that $AC=\sqrt{BC^2-AB^2}=\sqrt{13^2-9^2}=8\sqrt{2}$. Thus, $BE=\frac{1}{2}AC=4\sqrt{2}$ and $BC=\sqrt{2}BE$. Therefore, triangle $BEC$ is similar to triangle $BAC$ by RHS similarity, and is half its size. This gives us that triangles $BCX$ and $BXC$ will have the same area since line $CX$ is a median of triangle $BCX$. It follows that they share a base and have equal altitudes, so their areas are the same. This means that quadrilateral $AEXD$ also has the same area as triangle $BXC$. Therefore, the ratio of the area of $AEXD$ to the area of $BXC$ is $\boxed{1}$. [asy]
