Case 1:  If $(x,y)$ lies on the boundary line $x+y=3$, then we see from the graph that the line $x+y=3$ is adjacent to the red-dashed line $y=x$ only if $y>0$, so we substitute $x=3-y$ into the equation of $y=x$ to get $y=x=3-y \Rightarrow y=\frac{3}{2}$.  Therefore, $(x,y)=\left(\frac{3}{2},\frac{3}{2}\right)$.

We also see that $(x,y)$ lies on the boundary line of the blue-dashed line $2x+y=2$, so we substitute $y=2-2x$ into the equation of $y=x$ to get $y=x=2-2x\Rightarrow x=\frac{2}{3}$. Therefore, we can compute that  $(x,y)=\left(\frac{2}{3},\frac{2}{3}\right)$.

Case 2: If $(x,y)$ lies on boundary line of the other triangular region in the diagram, we see that the lines $x=0$ and $y=3-x$ intersect when $y=3-0=3$, so $(x,y)=(3,0)$.  Similarly, we see that $y=0$ and $2x+y=2$ intersect when $x=\frac{1}{2}$, so $(x,y)=\left(\frac{1}{2},0\right)$.

Therefore, we have the four vertices of the quadrilateral region: $\left(\frac{1}{2},0\right),\left(\frac{2}{3},\frac{2}{3}\right),\left(\frac{3}{2},\frac{3}{2}\right),\left(3,0\right)$.

We can determine which two of these vertices correspond to the largest distance by computing the distance between them.  If we draw a picture of these points on the coordinate system, we find that the distance between $\left(\frac{1}{2},0\right)$ and $\left(3,0\right)$ is larger, and we calculate that this distance is exactly $l=\root2\of{\frac{9}{4}-(\frac{1}{2})^2}=\frac{3\sqrt2}{2}$ units.  Therefore, the length of the longest side has $\boxed{3} \sqrt{2}$ units.

Note that from the coordinates of the four vertices, it is clear that the two unit vectors $\hat{i}+(1) \hat{j}$, and $2\hat{i}+(1) \hat{j}$  form the two original lines with which we were given. Therefore, the maximum distance from any vertex to any other vertex must be along the line segment connecting $(0,3)$ to $(3,0)$, and the answer given above is the length of that line segment.
