The natural logarithm is defined for positive numbers, so $\log{(3-x)}$ is defined only for $3 - x > 0,$ or in other words, when $x < 3.$

The number inside a square root is nonnegative, so $\sqrt{x - 1}$ is defined only for $x - 1 \ge 0,$ or in other words, when $x \ge 1.$

Combining these two conditions, it is clear that we must have $x < 3$ and $x \ge 1,$ so $\frac{\log{(3-x)}}{\sqrt{x-1}}$ is defined only for $1 \le x < 3.$  We have to exclude 1 for the same reason why we cannot divide by zero.
Answer: $x \in \boxed{(1,3)}.$

