accepted_response:
We define a monic third degree polynomial with integer coefficients that has $a,b,c$ as roots. Since $a,b,c$ are roots, $(x-a)(x-b)(x-c)=x^3-(a+b+c)x^2+(ab+ac+bc)x-abc$, so \begin{align*} x^3-(a+b+c)x^2+(ab+ac+bc)x-abc &= x^{3}-2x^{2}-7x + 14 \end{align*}

We want to find the largest real root of this polynomial. Observe that $x=2$ is a root, so $x-2$ is a factor. Using synthetic division on $x^{3}-2x^{2}-7x + 14$, we find that \begin{align*} x^3 - 2x^2 - 7x + 14 = (x-2)(x^2 - 7). \end{align*} The other two roots are $0$ and $\sqrt{7}$, so the largest of $a,b,c$ is $\boxed{\sqrt{7}}$.
