By the Law of Cosines,
\begin{align*}
BC^2 &= AB^2 + AC^2 - 2 \cdot AB \cdot AC \cdot \cos \angle A \\
&= 3^2 + 6^2 - 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 6 \cdot \frac{1}{8} \\
&= 45.
\end{align*}Hence, $BC = 3 \sqrt{5}.$

By the Angle Bisector Theorem,
\[\frac{CD}{BD} = \frac{AC}{AB} = 2.\]Also, $BC = BD + CD = 3 \sqrt{5},$ so $BD = \sqrt{5}$ and $CD = 2 \sqrt{5}.$

By the Law of Cosines,
\begin{align*}
AD^2 &= AB^2 + BD^2 - 2 \cdot AB \cdot BD \cdot \cos \angle A \\
&= 3^2 + (\sqrt{5})^2 - 2 \cdot 3 \cdot \sqrt{5} \cdot \frac{1}{8} \\
&= \frac{72}{8} = 9.
\end{align*}Hence, $AD = \boxed{3}.$

